  "toyota_summary": "The EU and China reached a preliminary deal today to restrict hybrid car imports, a move that gives European makers breathing room in their home market while signaling Brussels is willing to negotiate on trade rather than escalate. At the same time, center-right EU lawmakers are rebelling against the bloc's Industrial Accelerator Act, the flagship law meant to help European manufacturers compete globally, putting its future in doubt just as the industry faces intensifying Chinese competition.\n\nThe stakes are direct. European automakers need stable home-market protection as they transition to electric vehicles, which requires massive capital investment. A fractured EU industrial policy and a failed trade deal with China would leave them exposed on both fronts—undercut at home by cheaper Chinese hybrids and hamstrung by European rules that constrain their ability to scale. Separately, USMCA renegotiation under Trump's watch remains stuck in limbo, keeping North American supply chains uncertain.\n\nWatch whether the EU can hold its coalition together on the Industrial Accelerator Act, and whether Chinese makers use the Paris Motor Show this week to demonstrate they're already moving upmarket into premium segments.",
